-- title:
--   Normality of a local domain with principal-ideal quotient
-- statement:
--   Let $A$ be a commutative ring which is an integral domain, Noetherian, and local, and let $t \in A$ be an element such that the quotient $A/(t)$ by the ideal generated by $t$ is again an integral domain and is a principal ideal ring; thus $A/(t)$ is a principal ideal domain, i.e. a field or a discrete valuation ring (the case $t = 0$, where $A/(t) \cong A$, is permitted by the hypotheses). The conclusion is that $A$ is integrally closed, in the sense of Mathlib's `IsIntegrallyClosed`: every element of the fraction field of $A$ that is integral over $A$ lies in the image of $A$. No hypothesis relating $t$ to the maximal ideal of $A$, and no regularity or nonvanishing condition on $t$, is imposed beyond the two typeclass assumptions on $A/(t)$.
--
--   This is the cheap form of the assertion that normality lifts from a Cartier divisor: at a point where the fibre over $t$ is a principal ideal domain one need not invoke Serre's criterion, and the statement with $A/(t)$ merely normal is not asserted. It is used in the construction of integral models of modular curves, in [`ModularCurve.isIntegrallyClosed_modularLocalizedAtPoint_coeffSubring_of_pow_sq_ne`](thm.html#ModularCurve.isIntegrallyClosed_modularLocalizedAtPoint_coeffSubring_of_pow_sq_ne) and in the prolongation lemmas for place specialisations, to know that the relevant local rings of an arithmetic surface are integrally closed.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_IsLocalRing_isIntegrallyClosed_of_isPrincipalIdealRing_quotient.lean

import Mathlib

set_option maxHeartbeats 4000000
